<Base title="Data processing addendum · Comarket" description="How Comarket processes a brand's Stripe customer data as its processor: instructions, security, sub-processors, transfers and the standard contractual clauses." stage="none" noindex={draft}>
  <Doc
    title="Data processing addendum"
    lede="To decide whether a sale counts, we check buyers against your Stripe customers. For that, you are the controller and we are your processor. This addendum sets the rules."
    updated={updated}
    draft={draft}
    current="/dpa"
  >
    <h2>1. About this addendum</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">1.1</span>This Data Processing Addendum ("DPA") forms part of the <a href="/brand-terms">Brand Agreement</a>
      between [[LEGAL ENTITY NAME]] ("Comarket", "processor") and the brand ("Brand", "controller"). It applies whenever Comarket
      processes Brand Personal Data, and needs no separate signature. A Brand that wants a signed copy can ask at
      [[CONTACT EMAIL]].
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">1.2</span>If this DPA and the Brand Agreement conflict about personal data, this DPA applies. If this DPA and
      the Standard Contractual Clauses conflict, the Standard Contractual Clauses apply.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Definitions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Data Protection Law:</b> every law on personal data that applies to the processing, including the GDPR (Regulation (EU) 2016/679), the UK GDPR and Data Protection Act 2018, the Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection, and the California Consumer Privacy Act as amended by the CPRA (the "CCPA") and similar US state laws.</li>
      <li><b>Brand Personal Data:</b> personal data in the Customer Records, in any exclusion list, and about buyers who use a Storefront, that Comarket processes for the Brand under the Brand Agreement, as Annex 1 describes.</li>
      <li><b>controller, processor, data subject, personal data, personal data breach, processing and supervisory authority</b> have the meanings in the GDPR. Under the CCPA, "controller" means "business" and "processor" means "service provider".</li>
      <li><b>Sub-processor:</b> another processor Comarket engages to process Brand Personal Data.</li>
      <li><b>Standard Contractual Clauses</b> or <b>SCCs:</b> the clauses annexed to Commission Implementing Decision (EU) 2021/914.</li>
      <li><b>UK Addendum:</b> the International Data Transfer Addendum to the SCCs issued by the UK Information Commissioner under section 119A of the Data Protection Act 2018.</li>
      <li>Other words with capitals have the meanings in the Brand Agreement.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Roles</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.1</span>For Brand Personal Data, the Brand is the controller and Comarket is its processor. If the Brand is
      itself a processor for another controller, Comarket is its sub-processor, and the Brand confirms it has that controller's
      authority.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.2</span>Comarket is an independent controller, outside this DPA, for: information about the Brand's own
      staff who use Comarket; and its records of each Qualifying Sale (a reference that doesn't identify the buyer, dates,
      amounts, the creator credited, and the result of each check), which it keeps to bill the Brand, pay creators, meet tax
      and accounting law, prevent fraud and handle disputes. Those records contain no buyer email, phone number or card
      fingerprint.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">3.3</span>The Brand is responsible for having a lawful basis for the processing, and for telling buyers, in
      its privacy policy, that it uses service providers to check whether they are new customers and to credit referrals.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Comarket's duties as processor</h2>
    <p><span class="n">4.1</span>Comarket will:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>process Brand Personal Data only on the Brand's documented instructions, which are the Brand Agreement, this DPA and the Brand's settings in Comarket, unless the law requires otherwise, in which case it will tell the Brand first unless the law forbids that;</li>
      <li>tell the Brand at once if it believes an instruction breaks Data Protection Law;</li>
      <li>make sure everyone it authorises to process Brand Personal Data is bound to confidentiality;</li>
      <li>take the technical and organisational measures in Annex 2;</li>
      <li>follow section 5 when engaging Sub-processors;</li>
      <li>help the Brand, taking into account the nature of the processing, to answer data subjects exercising their rights, and pass on promptly any request it receives directly, without answering it except on the Brand's instructions;</li>
      <li>help the Brand with security, breach notifications, data protection impact assessments and prior consultations, as far as the information available to Comarket allows;</li>
      <li>at the end of the Brand Agreement, delete Brand Personal Data within 30 days (the Brand may ask for a copy of its Statements before then), unless the law requires Comarket to keep it, in which case it stays protected by this DPA; and</li>
      <li>make available the information needed to show it complies with this DPA, and allow audits under section 7.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      <span class="n">4.2</span><b>Data minimisation.</b> Comarket reads only the Customer Records it needs for each check. It does
      not keep a copy of the Brand's customer list. It keeps a buyer's email address, phone number and card fingerprint only
      until the check for that buyer's Attributed Checkout is complete. It keeps the Stripe customer and subscription IDs of a
      New Customer until the Commission Period, and the refund and dispute periods after it, have ended, so it can recognise
      Renewals and reversals, and then deletes them. It never shares Brand Personal Data with creators.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">4.3</span><b>CCPA.</b> Comarket will not: sell or share Brand Personal Data (as the CCPA uses those words);
      retain, use or disclose it for any purpose other than the business purposes in Annex 1, or outside the direct business
      relationship with the Brand; or combine it with personal data from other sources, except as the CCPA permits a service
      provider to. Comarket will comply with the CCPA, give the same level of protection it requires, and tell the Brand if it
      can no longer meet these duties. The Brand may take reasonable steps to stop and remedy unauthorised use. Comarket
      certifies that it understands these restrictions.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Sub-processors</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.1</span>The Brand gives Comarket general authorisation to engage Sub-processors. The current list is
      Annex 3.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.2</span>Comarket will tell the Brand by email at least 30 days before adding or replacing a Sub-processor.
      The Brand may object on reasonable data protection grounds within that time. If the parties can't resolve the
      objection, the Brand may end the Brand Agreement without penalty before the change takes effect; section 20.5 of the
      Brand Agreement still applies to sales already made.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.3</span>Comarket will impose on each Sub-processor, by contract, data protection duties that are no less
      protective than this DPA, and remains liable to the Brand for each Sub-processor's performance.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">5.4</span>Stripe is not a Sub-processor. Comarket reads the Brand's data from the Brand's own Stripe account,
      under the Brand's own agreement with Stripe and the access the Brand grants.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Personal data breaches</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">6.1</span>Comarket will tell the Brand without undue delay, and in any case within 48 hours, after becoming
      aware of a personal data breach affecting Brand Personal Data. It will describe the breach, the categories and
      approximate numbers of data subjects and records concerned, the likely consequences, and the measures taken or proposed,
      giving information in stages where it can't give it all at once. It will take reasonable steps to contain the breach
      and limit its effects. Telling the Brand is not an admission of fault.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Audits</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.1</span>Comarket will answer the Brand's reasonable written questions about its compliance, and share
      relevant summaries of its own and its Sub-processors' security certifications or audit reports.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">7.2</span>If that is not enough to show compliance, or a supervisory authority requires it, or after a
      personal data breach, the Brand may audit Comarket, itself or through an independent auditor bound by confidentiality,
      on at least 30 days' notice, during business hours, no more than once a year (except after a breach or where an
      authority requires it), and at its own cost. The audit must not give access to other customers' data.
    </p>

    <h2>8. International transfers</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">8.1</span>Comarket may transfer Brand Personal Data to a country outside the EEA, the UK or Switzerland only in
      line with Data Protection Law.
    </p>
    <p><span class="n">8.2</span>Where the transfer needs safeguards, the SCCs are incorporated into this DPA, as follows:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Module Two (controller to processor) applies where the Brand is the controller, and Module Three (processor to processor) where the Brand is a processor;</li>
      <li>where Comarket is established in the EEA and the Brand outside it, Module Four (processor to controller) applies to data Comarket sends back to the Brand, to the extent the SCCs require it;</li>
      <li>Clause 7 (docking clause) applies;</li>
      <li>Clause 9: Option 2 (general written authorisation), with the notice period in section 5.2;</li>
      <li>Clause 11: the optional wording doesn't apply;</li>
      <li>Clause 13: the supervisory authority is the one competent for the Brand under Clause 13(a);</li>
      <li>Clauses 17 and 18: the law and courts of [[SCC MEMBER STATE]];</li>
      <li>Annex I is Annex 1 of this DPA, Annex II is Annex 2 and Annex III is Annex 3.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <span class="n">8.3</span><b>UK.</b> For transfers subject to UK data protection law, the UK Addendum applies, completed with the
      information in this section and the Annexes. Table 4: either party may end it as Section 19 of the UK Addendum allows.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">8.4</span><b>Switzerland.</b> For transfers subject to Swiss law, the SCCs apply with these changes: the Swiss
      Federal Data Protection and Information Commissioner is the competent supervisory authority; references to the GDPR mean
      the Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection; and data subjects in Switzerland may bring claims in Switzerland.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">8.5</span>For onward transfers to Sub-processors, Comarket uses the SCCs, the UK Addendum, or an adequacy
      decision or framework such as the EU-US Data Privacy Framework where the recipient is certified.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">8.6</span>If a public authority asks Comarket for Brand Personal Data, Comarket will challenge requests it
      reasonably considers unlawful, disclose only what it is legally required to, and tell the Brand unless the law forbids it.
    </p>

    <h2>9. Liability and duration</h2>
    <p>
      <span class="n">9.1</span>Each party's liability under this DPA is subject to the limits in section 18 of the Brand Agreement,
      including the separate limit for data protection in 18.3, except where the SCCs or Data Protection Law don't allow a limit.
    </p>
    <p>
      <span class="n">9.2</span>This DPA lasts as long as Comarket processes Brand Personal Data.
    </p>

    <h2>Annex 1: details of the processing</h2>
    <div class="tbl">
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>Exporter</th><td>The Brand, as identified in its Comarket account. Role: controller (or processor). Contact: as in the account.</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Importer</th><td>[[LEGAL ENTITY NAME]], [[REGISTERED ADDRESS]]. Role: processor. Contact: [[PRIVACY EMAIL]].</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Data subjects</th><td>The Brand's customers in its Connected Stripe Account; people on the Brand's exclusion list; buyers who start an Attributed Checkout from a Storefront.</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Personal data</th><td>Email address; phone number; card fingerprint (a code Stripe derives from a card, never the card number); Stripe customer ID and the date the customer was created; for Attributed Checkouts, payment, subscription, refund and dispute IDs, dates, amounts, currency and status; the Storefront reference.</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Sensitive data</th><td>None. The Brand must not send special categories of data or data about criminal convictions.</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Frequency</th><td>Continuous, when a buyer starts an Attributed Checkout and when Stripe reports a payment, refund or dispute on it.</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Nature and purpose</th><td>Reading Customer Records through Stripe's API and event notifications; comparing identifiers to decide whether a buyer is new; recording the result; crediting sales to creators; calculating Creator Rates, Comarket Fees and reversals; preparing Statements. These are the business purposes under the CCPA.</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Duration</th><td>For the term of the Brand Agreement, plus deletion under 4.1(8). Buyer identifiers are kept only as section 4.2 says.</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Sub-processors</th><td>As Annex 3, for hosting, computing and storage.</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Supervisory authority</th><td>As in Clause 13 of the SCCs.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Annex 2: security measures</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Encryption:</b> TLS 1.2 or higher for all data in transit; encryption at rest in our database and backups.</li>
      <li><b>Access:</b> least privilege; named accounts with multi-factor authentication for every system holding Brand Personal Data; access removed promptly when no longer needed; secrets kept in our host's secret store, never in code.</li>
      <li><b>Stripe access:</b> the narrowest read permissions Stripe offers for the checks; Stripe event notifications verified by signature.</li>
      <li><b>Separation:</b> each Brand's data is kept logically separate, and every query is limited to one Brand.</li>
      <li><b>Minimisation:</b> identifiers kept only as section 4.2 says; Statements and creator dashboards use references that don't identify buyers.</li>
      <li><b>Logging and monitoring:</b> access to Brand Personal Data is logged; logs are kept for at least [[SECURITY LOG RETENTION]] and reviewed for misuse.</li>
      <li><b>Resilience:</b> managed infrastructure with backups and tested restores.</li>
      <li><b>Development:</b> code review before release; dependencies kept up to date; vulnerabilities fixed according to severity.</li>
      <li><b>Incident response:</b> a written plan for personal data breaches, including the notice in section 6.</li>
      <li><b>People:</b> confidentiality duties and data protection training for everyone with access.</li>
      <li><b>Sub-processors:</b> security and privacy checked before engagement and reviewed yearly.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Annex 3: sub-processors</h2>
    <div class="tbl">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Sub-processor</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Location</th><th>Transfer safeguard</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Cloudflare, Inc.</td>
            <td>Hosting and running Comarket's server code, caching and network security</td>
            <td>Global network; United States</td>
            <td>SCCs, or the EU-US Data Privacy Framework where certified</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Neon</td>
